// @test: differential
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0,1
// @tags: vbc, codegen, generics, mono, iterator, collect, regression
// @timeout: 120000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// len=5
// sum=10
// @expected-stdout-end
//
// THE GROUND WON BY T1214, pinned narrowly so it cannot be lost while
// the wider row is still open.
//
// `(0..5).collect()` aborted at Tier 1 with
//
//     AOT dispatch fault: method 'from_iter' has no compiled candidate
//     in this module (in `Range.collect`)
//
// and the reason was FOUR independent links, each of which had to be
// closed before the next became visible:
//
//   1. the receiver's NAME carried no type argument, so the
//      instantiation leg bound nothing (`Range` rather than
//      `Range<Int>` — an unsuffixed integer literal has no type name of
//      its own, and `literal_default_type_name` was consulted one link
//      too late);
//   2. the witness was read from type-parameter slot ZERO, always,
//      while `collect<C>`'s receiver `C` is slot 1;
//   3. the call site resolved by the method-name SUFFIX `.collect`,
//      which cannot match `Range.collect$mono$…`, so the specialisation
//      was never reached even though it was built, routed and lowered;
//   4. the runtime type switch inside `List.from_iter` admitted an arm
//      only if the candidate already had LLVM basic blocks — and bodies
//      lower in MODULE ORDER, so `Range.next` (IR line 114 702) had
//      none while `List.from_iter` (103 955) was being built.
//
// WHY A SEPARATE SPEC RATHER THAN A LINE IN THE EXISTING ONE.
// `collect-return-param-vs-variant-name.vr` is the row's acceptance and
// still fails: its SECOND `collect` — over a `MappedIter` — dies on a
// receiver whose runtime type id matches none of the switch's 347 arms,
// including the arm for `MappedIter` itself. That is a value-TAG
// question, a different family, and it will keep that spec red for as
// long as it takes. This one pins what already works, so a regression
// in any of the four links above is caught the day it happens instead
// of the day the wider row finally goes green.
//
// `sum` is not decoration: it drives the SAME `Range.next` dispatch
// through a different protocol default, so a fix that special-cases
// `collect` alone fails here.

mount core.prelude.*;

fn main() {
    let xs: List<Int> = (0..5).collect();
    print(f"len={xs.len()}");

    let s: Int = (1..=4).sum();
    print(f"sum={s}");
}
